WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.910
Sunday, July 11th, thousands of Cubans

00:00:02.910 --> 00:00:04.180
and dozens of cities

00:00:04.180 --> 00:00:06.420
around the Island Nation
took to the streets

00:00:06.420 --> 00:00:09.000
to protest the country's
communist dictatorship

00:00:09.000 --> 00:00:12.340
and persistent shortages in
food, energy, and medicine,

00:00:12.340 --> 00:00:14.890
all of which have been
made worse by the pandemic.

00:00:14.890 --> 00:00:17.400
The demonstrations have
been enabled by social media

00:00:17.400 --> 00:00:20.510
and the internet, which only
came to Cuba in a big way

00:00:20.510 --> 00:00:24.510
in late 2018 when
President Miguel Díaz-Canel

00:00:24.510 --> 00:00:28.410
allowed citizens access to data
plans on their cell phones.

00:00:28.410 --> 00:00:31.440
To better understand exactly
how Facebook, YouTube,

00:00:31.440 --> 00:00:34.980
WhatsApp, and other social
media platforms are connecting

00:00:34.980 --> 00:00:36.640
and empowering the Cuban people

00:00:36.640 --> 00:00:38.550
and undermining state control,

00:00:38.550 --> 00:00:40.330
Reason spoke with Ted Henken,

00:00:40.330 --> 00:00:43.480
a sociologist at Baruch
College and the co-editor

00:00:43.480 --> 00:00:46.587
of the collection, 'Cuba's
Digital Revolution'.

00:00:47.730 --> 00:00:51.120
Ted Henken, thanks for talking to Reason

00:00:51.120 --> 00:00:52.870
and the first question I'm gonna ask is,

00:00:52.870 --> 00:00:57.870
how is the internet enabling
what's going on in Cuba?

00:00:59.510 --> 00:01:02.270
Yeah, okay, I think
it's important to start

00:01:02.270 --> 00:01:06.180
with what's not happening
and that is important

00:01:06.180 --> 00:01:10.320
because we tend to read foreign
developments as they relate

00:01:10.320 --> 00:01:14.950
to the internet, shallowly, and examples

00:01:14.950 --> 00:01:17.670
of that happened a lot with Arab Spring

00:01:17.670 --> 00:01:22.030
and calling those things
Twitter or Facebook revolutions.

00:01:22.030 --> 00:01:25.260
And I think what's important
to understand in that sense

00:01:25.260 --> 00:01:27.750
in general and then Cuba specifically is

00:01:27.750 --> 00:01:32.583
that the internet is acting
as a facilitator of interests

00:01:36.230 --> 00:01:39.600
and groups and protests and discontent

00:01:39.600 --> 00:01:43.830
that traditionally were silenced
and didn't have any place

00:01:43.830 --> 00:01:47.850
or channel where they could
really express themselves,

00:01:47.850 --> 00:01:50.400
so they're not internet
revolutions, I think it's also,

00:01:50.400 --> 00:01:54.210
it's important to call those
internet enabled uprisings

00:01:54.210 --> 00:01:55.623
or protest movements, right?

00:01:57.900 --> 00:02:01.680
There's an old concept called
the Dictator's Dilemma,

00:02:01.680 --> 00:02:02.940
which is any technology

00:02:02.940 --> 00:02:07.210
that may enter an authoritarian system

00:02:07.210 --> 00:02:12.210
as a double-edged sword and
could help the dictator rule,

00:02:12.580 --> 00:02:15.960
maybe legitimize the dictator
or the authoritarian leader

00:02:15.960 --> 00:02:19.410
because of education or development, or,

00:02:19.410 --> 00:02:22.800
you know, any kind of other
things that this technology,

00:02:22.800 --> 00:02:24.740
in this case the internet enables,

00:02:24.740 --> 00:02:27.150
but it's a double-edged
sword in that it can be used

00:02:27.150 --> 00:02:30.320
for a lot of other means by other actors

00:02:30.320 --> 00:02:32.400
and this is certainly the case in Cuba

00:02:32.400 --> 00:02:36.060
and so that Dictator's Dilemma,

00:02:36.060 --> 00:02:37.880
although sometimes it was read

00:02:37.880 --> 00:02:42.880
from a perspective of technological
determination, you know,

00:02:42.930 --> 00:02:45.730
whenever internet enters
a society, game over,

00:02:45.730 --> 00:02:50.730
and Wael Ghonim, the
former Facebook executive

00:02:51.480 --> 00:02:56.480
who was Egyptian, you know,
regretted his overly rosy idea

00:02:57.850 --> 00:03:02.520
that you add internet
and the regime is over.

00:03:02.520 --> 00:03:04.240
And he learned the hard way that yes,

00:03:04.240 --> 00:03:07.980
the internet and social
media is useful and helpful

00:03:07.980 --> 00:03:12.980
to undermine monopoly control
of media communications

00:03:13.060 --> 00:03:16.690
and other aspects that are
fundamentally important

00:03:16.690 --> 00:03:19.190
for totalitarian systems,

00:03:19.190 --> 00:03:21.140
whether of the right or the left,

00:03:21.140 --> 00:03:24.510
but they don't declare a winner,

00:03:24.510 --> 00:03:26.830
they change the game in important ways.

00:03:26.830 --> 00:03:30.360
Can you take a step back and explain why,

00:03:30.360 --> 00:03:33.388
how did the internet come to Cuba?

00:03:33.388 --> 00:03:37.550
You've written about how in
December, 2018, you know,

00:03:37.550 --> 00:03:40.030
basically data plans were allowed

00:03:40.030 --> 00:03:43.200
for phone service or 3G Mobile.

00:03:43.200 --> 00:03:44.650
Why did that happen?

00:03:44.650 --> 00:03:48.530
Well, that's a head-scratcher because,

00:03:48.530 --> 00:03:50.910
and it goes back to the
Dictator's Dilemma, I mean,

00:03:50.910 --> 00:03:55.910
before that you basically
have a period in the 90s

00:03:55.940 --> 00:03:58.410
when Cuba was actually pretty advanced

00:03:58.410 --> 00:04:02.000
in the region, in Latin
America and the Caribbean

00:04:02.000 --> 00:04:05.350
in terms of its networking and internet

00:04:05.350 --> 00:04:08.900
and that kind of thing, but Fidel

00:04:08.900 --> 00:04:13.900
and the leaders at that time
realized that this could be a,

00:04:14.280 --> 00:04:18.110
they called it a wild colt
that needed to be tamed, right?

00:04:18.110 --> 00:04:22.630
And they saw it as a mortal threat

00:04:23.710 --> 00:04:27.060
because it undermined
monopoly information control

00:04:27.060 --> 00:04:29.080
which is one of the fundamental hallmarks

00:04:29.080 --> 00:04:31.180
of the Cuban system.

00:04:31.180 --> 00:04:35.300
The mass media is propaganda and Cuba,

00:04:35.300 --> 00:04:39.160
there's no two ways about
it and almost everyone right

00:04:39.160 --> 00:04:44.160
or left who understands and
studies Cuba would grant that

00:04:45.600 --> 00:04:48.120
and so the internet in
that sense, you know,

00:04:48.120 --> 00:04:49.780
has a revolutionary,

00:04:49.780 --> 00:04:53.783
or you could say a counter
revolutionary potential,

00:04:55.490 --> 00:04:59.270
but because of under development,
because of priorities,

00:04:59.270 --> 00:05:01.470
and because of that fear,
and you could even add

00:05:01.470 --> 00:05:04.470
because of the U.S. embargo
isolating and cutting Cuba off

00:05:04.470 --> 00:05:09.083
from these kinds of communication
influences, technologies,

00:05:09.940 --> 00:05:12.550
because it was part of
the overall embargo,

00:05:12.550 --> 00:05:15.283
Cuba isolated itself and was isolated.

00:05:17.010 --> 00:05:21.720
That gradually ended when
Raul Castro became president

00:05:21.720 --> 00:05:25.300
in 2008 and gradually thereafter
Cubans started getting,

00:05:25.300 --> 00:05:27.590
gradually, but very gradually online,

00:05:27.590 --> 00:05:29.040
they started getting cell phones,

00:05:29.040 --> 00:05:31.620
they started to be able
to buy and use laptops.

